From: portare-ch <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] libretro: pin EE, VU and GS to the fastest cores, in a fixed order

Thread pinning was off: the defaults that turn it on for big.LITTLE
CPUs are written only when the ini is first created, which the libretro
core's settings path never did. EE, VU and GS moved between the big
cores, each move refilling a private L2, and the scheduler sometimes
stacked all three on one core.

The libretro core now turns pinning on, and the processor list it pins
from is ranked as the kernel ranks cores: cpu_capacity, else
cpuinfo_max_freq, else cpuinfo's own frequency, using the reader the
Android path already had. cpuinfo's frequency read 0 for every core on
the 8 Gen 2 under Linux, and ranking by it put EE, VU and GS on the
A510s. The order is total: within a tier of equal weight, the newer
design, then the core number. On the 8 Gen 2 that is X3, A715, A715,
A710, A710, A510s: EE gets the prime core to itself, VU and GS the
A715s.

2.8 builds the capacity reader on all of Linux itself, so only the
ranking and the libretro side are carried here.
---
diff --git a/pcsx2-libretro/Main.cpp b/pcsx2-libretro/Main.cpp
--- a/pcsx2-libretro/Main.cpp
+++ b/pcsx2-libretro/Main.cpp
@@ -268,6 +268,13 @@ bool LibretroCore::InitializeConfig()
 		// is two frames of input lag. At 0 the EE starts a frame once the
 		// last one is taken, as the standalone ran.
 		s_base_settings->SetIntValue("EmuCore/GS", "VsyncQueueSize", 0);
+
+		// EE, VU and GS each on a core of their own, fastest first: on a
+		// Snapdragon 8 Gen 2 the X3 for EE alone, the A715s for VU and GS.
+		// The defaults would turn this on for any big.LITTLE CPU, but they
+		// are only written when the ini is created, and threads that move
+		// between cores refill a private L2 each time.
+		s_base_settings->SetBoolValue("EmuCore", "EnableThreadPinning", true);
 	}
 
 	Error save_error;
diff --git a/pcsx2/VMManager.cpp b/pcsx2/VMManager.cpp
--- a/pcsx2/VMManager.cpp
+++ b/pcsx2/VMManager.cpp
@@ -4447,7 +4447,9 @@ static std::vector<u32> GetProcessorWeights()
 	}
 	return weights;
 }
+#endif
 
+#if defined(__ANDROID__)
 // Every processor within PERF_CLUSTER_RATIO of the fastest one. Deliberately NOT "the cluster
 // owning the fastest core": cpuinfo starts a new cluster whenever max_frequency differs, so on
 // any 1+3+4 or 1+2+2+3 flagship the prime core is a cluster of ONE, and confining EE/VU/GS to
@@ -4586,10 +4588,30 @@ static void InitializeProcessorList()
 		processors.push_back(proc);
 	}
 
-	// Prioritize faster cores in heterogeneous CPUs.
+	// Prioritize faster cores in heterogeneous CPUs, ranked as the kernel ranks them:
+	// cpu_capacity, else cpuinfo_max_freq, else cpuinfo's own frequency (GetProcessorWeights).
+	// cpuinfo's frequency alone read 0 for every core on a Snapdragon 8 Gen 2 under Linux, and
+	// pinning then put EE, VU and GS on the A510s. Pinning takes the first three, so the order has
+	// to be total: a tier of equal weight can mix designs (that SoC's two A715s and two A710s are
+	// all 657), and within one, cpuinfo numbers a newer design of the same series higher. Only
+	// within a tier: across series the numbering means nothing (the X3 sorts below the A510).
+	// The core number settles the rest.
+#if defined(__linux__)
+	const std::vector<u32> weights = GetProcessorWeights();
+	const cpuinfo_processor* const first = cpuinfo_get_processors();
+	const auto weight = [&weights, first](const cpuinfo_processor* proc) {
+		return weights[static_cast<size_t>(proc - first)];
+	};
+#else
+	const auto weight = [](const cpuinfo_processor* proc) { return proc->core->frequency; };
+#endif
 	std::sort(processors.begin(), processors.end(),
-		[](const cpuinfo_processor* lhs, const cpuinfo_processor* rhs) {
-			return (lhs->core->frequency > rhs->core->frequency);
+		[&weight](const cpuinfo_processor* lhs, const cpuinfo_processor* rhs) {
+			if (weight(lhs) != weight(rhs))
+				return (weight(lhs) > weight(rhs));
+			if (weight(lhs) != 0 && lhs->core->uarch != rhs->core->uarch)
+				return (lhs->core->uarch > rhs->core->uarch);
+			return (GetProcessorIdForProcessor(lhs) < GetProcessorIdForProcessor(rhs));
 		});
 
 	SmallString str;
